WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.014
Batch tuning without measurement quickly becomes guesswork.

2
00:00:04.014 --> 00:00:08.331
Start with a repeatable benchmark that reflects the real workload.

3
00:00:08.331 --> 00:00:14.844
Use consistent prompts, fixed context lengths, the same model and quantization, and a quiet machine.

4
00:00:14.844 --> 00:00:19.918
Record the runtime version and hardware state so you can explain results later.

5
00:00:19.918 --> 00:00:25.749
A quick test can be useful, but a repeatable test is what lets you compare settings honestly.

6
00:00:25.749 --> 00:00:27.643
Measure more than one number.

7
00:00:27.643 --> 00:00:33.095
Prompt processing speed tells you how quickly the runtime handles existing context.

8
00:00:33.095 --> 00:00:39.230
Generation speed, often reported as tokens per second, tells you how quickly new tokens appear.

9
00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:43.319
First-token latency tells you how responsive the system feels.

10
00:00:43.319 --> 00:00:47.333
Total response time shows what the user experiences end to end.

11
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:51.195
For services, also track requests per second and queue time.

12
00:00:51.195 --> 00:00:54.452
Watch resource behavior while the benchmark runs.

13
00:00:54.452 --> 00:01:02.934
GPU utilization, CPU utilization, VRAM use, system RAM, temperatures, and throttling can explain why a setting improved or failed.

14
00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:08.917
A result that looks fast during a cool one-minute test may degrade after the hardware heats up.

15
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:14.748
Background tasks, driver activity, caching, and warmup effects can also distort results.

16
00:01:14.748 --> 00:01:17.778
Change one setting at a time whenever possible.

17
00:01:17.778 --> 00:01:27.017
If you increase batch size, change context length, and raise concurrency in the same test, you may get a number but you will not know what caused it.

18
00:01:27.017 --> 00:01:30.879
Keep a simple table of settings, metrics, and observations.

19
00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:36.938
The discipline of measurement is what turns tuning from folklore into an engineering process.

20
00:01:36.938 --> 00:01:42.164
For team use, write down the benchmark as if someone else will repeat it next month.

21
00:01:42.164 --> 00:01:52.160
Include the exact prompt shape, generation limit, runtime version, model file, quantization, hardware, driver version, and the settings being compared.

22
00:01:52.160 --> 00:02:01.324
The notes do not need to be elaborate, but they should be specific enough to prevent a future tuning conversation from becoming guesswork again.
